---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import PNotes from "../components/PNotes.astro";
import octo from "../img/octo-multi.png";
---

<p-slide layout="center" autoplay timing="550">
  <h2 gradient style="margin-block-end: 0.2em">
    What is Multithreading?
  </h2>
  <p-fragment>
    <Image
      src={octo}
      alt="An octopus juggling many tasks at once"
      style="--size: 6em"
    />
  </p-fragment>
</p-slide>
<p-slide layout="center">
  <figure>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        In computer architecture, multithreading is the ability of a central
        processing unit (CPU) (or a single core in a multi-core processor)
        <b>to provide multiple threads</b> of execution.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <figcaption>
      <cite>
        <a
          href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multithreading_(computer_architecture)"
          target="_blank"
        >
          https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multithreading_(computer_architecture)
        </a>
      </cite>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</p-slide>
<p-slide cover layout="center">
  <div gradient="sub">
    <h3 style="margin-block-end: 0">And what about thread management in</h3>
    <h3 style="margin-block-start: 1rem">Web Browsers?</h3>
  </div>
  <span aria-hidden="true">🤔</span>
</p-slide>
<p-slide layout="center">
  <p>
    Web browsers are complex applications that handle a variety of tasks
    simultaneously
  </p>
  <div
    layout="grid"
    text="mid"
    style="margin-block-start: 1em; margin-inline: auto; text-align: start"
  >
    <p-fragment p-effect="from left">👉 Rendering web pages</p-fragment>
    <p-fragment p-effect="from right">👉 Executing JavaScript</p-fragment>
    <p-fragment p-effect="zoom">👉 Managing network requests</p-fragment>
    <p-fragment p-effect="from below">👉 Handling user input</p-fragment>
  </div>
  <PNotes>
    Thread Management in Web Browsers Web browsers are complex applications that
    handle a variety of tasks simultaneously, such as rendering web pages,
    executing JavaScript, managing network requests, and handling user input. To
    manage these tasks efficiently, modern web browsers use a combination of
    single-threaded and multi-threaded architectures. The list is not
    exhaustive, but it highlights the importance of thread management in web
    browsers.
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h3 layout="center" with-emoji>
    <span>Single-Threaded</span> <span aria-hidden="true">🛣</span>
  </h3>
  <p-fragment p-effect="from right">📍 JavaScript Execution</p-fragment>
  <p-fragment p-effect="from left">📍 Event Handling</p-fragment>
  <p-fragment p-effect="zoom">📍 Rendering</p-fragment>
  <p-fragment p-effect="from below">📍 DOM Manipulation</p-fragment>
  <PNotes>
    All of these run on the main thread. Rendering here means style, layout and
    paint; rasterization and compositing happen on other threads.
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h3 layout="center" with-emoji>
    <span>Multi-Threaded</span> <span aria-hidden="true">🛣 🛣</span>
  </h3>
  <p-fragment p-effect="zoom">🚀 Networking</p-fragment>
  <p-fragment p-effect="from below">🚀 GPU Acceleration</p-fragment>
  <p-fragment p-effect="from right">
    🚀 HTML5 APIs (WebRTC, Web Audio API, File API)
  </p-fragment>
  <p-fragment p-effect="from left">
    🚀 <b>Web worker</b>
  </p-fragment>
  <PNotes>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Networking: Internally, these use multiple threads for handling network
        I/O.
      </li>
      <li>
        GPU: rasterization and compositing run off the main thread (compositor
        thread, raster threads, GPU process)
      </li>
      <li>
        HTML5 APIs: APIs like WebRTC, Web Audio API, and File API often use
        multiple threads internally
      </li>
      <li>Web worker: each Web Worker runs on its own OS-level thread</li>
    </ul>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h3 with-emoji style="--justify-content: flex-start">
    <span aria-hidden="true">🌰</span> <span>In a nutshell</span>
  </h3>
  <p p-fragment p-effect="from below" text="mid" style="margin-block-end: 2rem">
    Web browsers employ a hybrid approach to thread management.
  </p>
  <p-fragment text="mid">
    <p style="margin-block-end: 1rem">
      📣 <b>Single-Threaded</b>: JavaScript execution on the main thread,
      managed by an event loop.
    </p>
    <p>
      📣 <b>Multi-Threaded</b>: Use of Web Workers for background tasks, and
      multi-threaded browser engine components for rendering, networking, and
      GPU acceleration.
    </p>
  </p-fragment>
</p-slide>
